			<div class="content" id="post-content-4856766">
									

<p>Here was my review from the Art Exchange forum:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><em>This</em> is a delightfully disturbing real-feeling story. I want to say very little about it so as not to spoil it. I do think that in the climax, the laughing felt out of place and out of character. There was also a description of something becoming louder despite the fact it's established the cameras don't have sound detection. But, yeah. Captivating. Disturbing. I will have more to say when this is posted &#8212; and it had <em>better</em> be posted, because <em>damn.</em> Oh, and while I don't think the SCP Foundation would <em>ever</em> have content warnings, I support the execution the author used here, keeping the content warning in-universe. Despite it feeling out-of-character for the Foundation, it aids in immersion, so if you must have a content warning in an SCP article, I do recommend this method.</p>
<p>Alright fine I decided I have more to say. This almost felt like one of those skips that was a glorified tale, where the anomaly isn't <em>really</em> necessary for the story and in fact is just a framework so that the author can write their tale idea in clinical tone and on the mainlist where it will get more attention. However, the ending pulled it back around, and I really appreciate that. Okay, <em>now</em> I'm done.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My opinion remains unchanged. Good, disturbing shit. Oh, and thanks for taking my critique into consideration!</p>

